  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` now handles 200,000 orders well under a second. The output is unchanged, and `npm test` passes with all 4 tests.\n\n**Why it was slow:** every order triggered three expensive steps:\n- a linear `seen.includes` scan for duplicates,\n- a linear `groups.find` scan for the customer,\n- a full re-sort of all groups.\n\n**What changed:**\n- Duplicate order IDs are now tracked in a `Set`.\n- Customer groups are kept in a `Map`.\n- The ranking sort runs once at the end instead of after every order. This gives the same order because `byRank` never ties: it falls back to `customerId`, which is unique per group.\n\n**Tests added in `test/report.test.js`:**\n- **Equivalence test:** it keeps the original implementation as a reference and compares full output with `deepStrictEqual` on 50 seeded random datasets at three limits. The data includes duplicate IDs, refunds, EUR orders and tied spend or first-order times, so a change in ordering or values would fail it.\n- **Speed test:** it runs 200,000 orders and fails if the call takes 1 second or more.\n\nI did not time the 200,000-order case separately. It passed the speed test, which allows up to 1 second.\n\nOne edge case differs in theory: a customer ID of `NaN` would now group together, where the old code treated each one as separate. Real customer IDs are strings, so I left it.",
